# Seven notes: three shown to the default reader (the Horowitz-Polchinski solution, the weak-coupling Hagedorn
# transition the paper compares with, and the Gregory-Laflamme instability), four for students only. Three sit in
# the introduction; the others follow the first body paragraph that works with the idea. Listed in reading order.
BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-hagedorn", "after": "sec-intro-p1", "title": "The thermal circle and the Hagedorn temperature", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A thermal partition function $\operatorname{Tr}e^{-\beta H}$ is a Euclidean path integral with time periodic, of length $\beta=1/T$: space times a thermal circle. A closed string can wrap this circle. The wrapped string's mass squared is its stretching energy, which grows with the circle's radius $R$, minus a zero-point energy. On a small enough circle, at a high enough temperature, the zero-point term wins and the wrapped string is tachyonic. The radius $R_H$ at which it is massless defines the Hagedorn temperature, beyond which the canonical partition function of a gas of strings diverges, a sign of the exponential growth of the number of string states with energy. Just colder than this temperature, for $R-R_H\ll l_s$, the wrapped string is a light scalar field $\chi$ in the theory reduced on the circle, with $m^2\propto R-R_H$. The string star is a bound state of this light field with gravity.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-ads-bh", "after": "sec-intro-p5", "title": "Black holes in AdS and the Hawking–Page transition", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In anti de Sitter space of radius $l_{ads}$, the Euclidean partition function at temperature $1/\beta$ receives contributions from thermal AdS and from AdS-Schwarzschild black holes. For every $\beta$ below $2\pi l_{ads}/\sqrt{d(d-2)}$ there are two black holes. The large one has a horizon bigger than $l_{ads}$ and positive specific heat; above the Hawking–Page temperature, of order $1/l_{ads}$, its free energy is lower than thermal AdS's, so it dominates. This is the Hawking–Page transition. The small black hole has a horizon smaller than $l_{ads}$ and behaves like a flat-space Schwarzschild black hole, with negative specific heat and positive free energy, so it never dominates. By holography these saddles are phases of the dual CFT on a sphere: thermal AdS is the confined phase, with free energy of order one, and the large black hole the deconfined phase, with free energy of order $N^2$. The small black hole is a metastable phase whose fate at the string scale is the paper's concern.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-weak-hagedorn", "after": "sec-intro-p7", "title": "The Hagedorn transition in weakly coupled gauge theory", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A weakly coupled large-$N$ gauge theory on a sphere can be reduced to a matrix model for the holonomy $U$ of the gauge field around the thermal circle, by integrating out all other modes. In the confined phase the eigenvalues of $U$ are spread uniformly around the circle and $u_1=\operatorname{Tr}U/N$, the Polyakov loop, vanishes. At the Hagedorn temperature the coefficient of $|u_1|^2$ in the effective action changes sign, and the uniform distribution becomes unstable. Near that point the effective action is $a\,\delta T\,|u_1|^2+b\,|u_1|^4$, whose extremum away from $u_1=0$ is a phase with a non-uniform but ungapped eigenvalue distribution, and free energy scaling as $\delta T^2$. Whether this phase is stable depends on the sign of $b$, which needs higher-loop computations. Following it further, the distribution develops a gap through a third-order Gross–Wadia–Witten transition. At strong coupling the analogous branch is expected to be the small AdS black hole, and the paper's string star reproduces the $\delta T^2$ scaling of the free energy.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-gl", "after": "sec-thermo-p6", "title": "The Gregory–Laflamme instability", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A black string, a black hole extended along a direction much longer than its horizon radius $r_h$, is classically unstable: perturbations varying along the string as $e^{ikz}$, with $k$ below a critical value of order $1/r_h$, grow exponentially in time (Gregory and Laflamme, 1993). Thermodynamics anticipates this: when the direction is compact and long, a black hole localized in all directions has more entropy than the uniform string of the same mass. In the Euclidean picture the threshold mode is static, a zero mode of the action, and the longer-wavelength ones are negative modes, inherited from the negative mode of the lower-dimensional black hole. A small AdS black hole in $AdS_5\times S^5$ is such a black string smeared over the $S^5$; when its horizon is much smaller than $l_{ads}$ it is unstable towards a black hole localized on the sphere, a ten-dimensional black hole. The paper looks for the same structure, the Gregory–Laflamme instability, in the string star.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hp", "after": "sec-small_sol-p1", "title": "The Horowitz–Polchinski solution", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>For the Horowitz–Polchinski solution, Horowitz and Polchinski reduced string theory on the thermal circle just below the Hagedorn temperature, keeping the light winding scalar $\chi$, with $m^2\propto(R-R_H)/R_H$ in string units, and the Newtonian potential $\varphi$, the fluctuation of the circle's size. Because the winding mass falls where the circle shrinks, $\chi$ sources a negative $\varphi$, which lowers its own mass: the condensate attracts itself. A bound state exists when this attraction balances the gradient and mass terms. Scaling the equations, the solution has size $L\sim l_s((R-R_H)/R_H)^{-1/2}$, amplitude of order $(R-R_H)/R_H$, and entropy of order $(l_s^{d-1}/G_N)((R-R_H)/R_H)^{(4-d)/2}$. Normalizable solutions exist only for $3\le d\le5$ spatial dimensions. Extrapolated to $R-R_H\sim l_s$, the size and entropy match those of a string-sized black hole: the correspondence principle in canonical form. The paper's cold AdS star is this solution.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-negative", "after": "sec-therm_inst-p2", "title": "Negative modes of Euclidean saddles", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Around a saddle of the Euclidean action, the path integral is approximated by a Gaussian integral over fluctuations, weighted by the second variation of the action. If that second variation has a negative eigenvalue on a normalizable fluctuation, a negative mode, the Gaussian integral diverges and must be defined by rotating the contour; the saddle then gives the free energy an imaginary part, the signature of an instability, as a bounce does for tunnelling. Gross, Perry and Yaffe found such a mode for the Euclidean Schwarzschild black hole: a change of the horizon size at fixed asymptotic temperature. It is tied to the black hole's negative specific heat, since the free energy at fixed temperature is then a maximum rather than a minimum in the black hole's size. A thermodynamically stable saddle has no negative mode. The paper asks whether the string star has one.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-rt", "after": "sec-ee-p2", "title": "Holographic entanglement entropy", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>For a state of a holographic CFT and a region $A$ of space, Ryu and Takayanagi proposed that the entanglement entropy of $A$ is the area of the extremal bulk surface $X$ ending on the boundary of $A$, divided by $4G_N$. Lewkowycz and Maldacena derived this from the replica trick: $\operatorname{Tr}\rho_A^n$ is the action of a bulk saddle whose boundary has $n$ copies glued along $A$, and as $n\to1$ the variation of that action localizes on $X$. Faulkner, Lewkowycz and Maldacena added the first quantum correction, the entanglement entropy of the bulk fields in the region $a$ between $X$ and $A$, of order one rather than $1/G_N$. For bulk matter in a thermal state, that term is the matter's thermal entropy in $a$. The paper reads its holographic entanglement entropy this way: the string star's thermal entropy, of order $1/G_N$, adds to the area term.</p>"""},
]
